1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:05,733
Hello, I am presenting my Everfit Workout Logging API.

2
00:00:05,733 --> 00:00:13,250
It helps coaches record client workouts, review history, and compare personal records.

3
00:00:14,642 --> 00:00:22,492
I will explain the architecture, demonstrate every API, and show important error cases.

4
00:00:22,492 --> 00:00:27,946
Then I will explain my AI workflow and walk through the record calculation

5
00:00:27,946 --> 00:00:29,625
code line by line.

6
00:00:31,017 --> 00:00:37,067
I will finish with the changes I would consider for ten thousand concurrent

7
00:00:37,067 --> 00:00:37,533
coaches.

8
00:00:38,850 --> 00:00:45,066
I chose NestJS for clear module boundaries and dependency injection.

9
00:00:45,066 --> 00:00:49,733
Controllers handle HTTP requests.

10
00:00:49,733 --> 00:00:52,933
Zod validates input.

11
00:00:52,933 --> 00:01:00,833
Services coordinate business operations, and repositories contain parameterized SQL.

12
00:01:02,225 --> 00:01:10,425
PostgreSQL gives this API transactions, relational constraints, and reliable aggregation.

13
00:01:10,425 --> 00:01:17,358
I use the pg driver directly, so the transaction boundaries and query plans

14
00:01:17,358 --> 00:01:18,425
remain visible.

15
00:01:19,808 --> 00:01:23,308
The main relationship is simple.

16
00:01:23,308 --> 00:01:29,624
Each workout entry belongs to a user identifier and an exercise.

17
00:01:29,624 --> 00:01:33,474
Each entry contains ordered sets.

18
00:01:33,474 --> 00:01:40,563
A set stores repetitions, the original weight and unit, and a normalized kilogram

19
00:01:40,563 --> 00:01:41,108
value.

20
00:01:42,475 --> 00:01:47,675
Exercises and muscle groups have a separate mapping table.

21
00:01:47,675 --> 00:01:52,925
This keeps muscle group metadata outside business logic.

22
00:01:52,925 --> 00:01:59,508
There is no users table or authentication in the assignment implementation.

23
00:02:00,892 --> 00:02:07,592
For history, an index begins with user ID, then workout time and entry

24
00:02:07,592 --> 00:02:08,058
ID.

25
00:02:08,058 --> 00:02:12,942
A second index also includes exercise ID.

26
00:02:12,942 --> 00:02:18,092
These support filtering and stable cursor pagination.

27
00:02:19,475 --> 00:02:23,041
Weight conversion is centralized.

28
00:02:23,041 --> 00:02:29,541
One pound equals zero point four five three five nine two three seven

29
00:02:29,541 --> 00:02:30,041
kilograms.

30
00:02:30,041 --> 00:02:37,925
Database numeric values and decimal arithmetic avoid binary floating point calculations.

31
00:02:37,925 --> 00:02:43,158
Display values are rounded only at the response boundary.

32
00:02:44,558 --> 00:02:48,008
Timestamps use UTC.

33
00:02:48,008 --> 00:02:52,924
A date range includes its start and excludes its end.

34
00:02:52,924 --> 00:02:58,441
This lets adjacent months meet without counting the boundary twice.

35
00:02:59,808 --> 00:03:04,591
CSV processing runs in a separate worker.

36
00:03:04,591 --> 00:03:12,082
PostgreSQL stores its jobs and progress, so recovery does not depend on API

37
00:03:12,082 --> 00:03:12,658
memory.

38
00:03:14,017 --> 00:03:19,600
I chose this approach to keep the assignment operationally simple.

39
00:03:19,600 --> 00:03:26,455
It still introduces storage and memory costs, which I will discuss when covering

40
00:03:26,455 --> 00:03:26,983
scale.

41
00:03:28,350 --> 00:03:33,566
First, the health endpoint checks database connectivity.

42
00:03:33,566 --> 00:03:39,366
A healthy response does not prove that the import worker is running.

43
00:03:40,767 --> 00:03:47,717
Next, the exercise catalog supports pagination and a muscle group filter.

44
00:03:47,717 --> 00:03:54,933
Workout logging resolves names against this catalog and rejects unknown exercises.

45
00:03:56,308 --> 00:04:01,341
This single request records Bench Press in January.

46
00:04:01,341 --> 00:04:07,704
It contains one set at sixty kilograms for eight repetitions, and another at

47
00:04:07,704 --> 00:04:10,641
one hundred pounds for five repetitions.

48
00:04:10,641 --> 00:04:16,808
The response preserves the original values and includes normalized weights.

49
00:04:18,183 --> 00:04:23,316
Every JSON write requires an idempotency key.

50
00:04:23,316 --> 00:04:28,516
I now send exactly the same request with the same key.

51
00:04:28,516 --> 00:04:35,283
The API returns the original result, including the same entry ID.

52
00:04:36,642 --> 00:04:41,258
Next, I change the payload but keep that key.

53
00:04:41,258 --> 00:04:45,008
The API returns a conflict.

54
00:04:45,008 --> 00:04:50,275
It cannot silently change what an earlier request meant.

55
00:04:51,642 --> 00:04:57,608
This bulk request records February Bench Press and Squat together.

56
00:04:57,608 --> 00:05:02,025
Both entries share one database transaction.

57
00:05:02,025 --> 00:05:07,175
If any entry fails, the entire request rolls back.

58
00:05:08,558 --> 00:05:13,774
The retry reservation and workout data commit together.

59
00:05:13,774 --> 00:05:21,391
A database unique constraint coordinates requests even across separate API processes.

60
00:05:21,391 --> 00:05:26,441
A different key intentionally represents a new submission.

61
00:05:26,441 --> 00:05:32,874
The same exercise and timestamp alone are not enough to identify a retry.

62
00:05:34,267 --> 00:05:40,167
The response comparison is based on status and JSON content.

63
00:05:40,167 --> 00:05:45,167
It does not require identical object-key ordering.

64
00:05:45,167 --> 00:05:52,138
This matters because PostgreSQL stores the response as JSONB, which can reorder keys

65
00:05:52,138 --> 00:05:54,283
without changing their meaning.

66
00:05:55,683 --> 00:06:03,669
History supports an exercise name substring, muscle group, date range, and requested output

67
00:06:03,669 --> 00:06:04,233
unit.

68
00:06:04,233 --> 00:06:10,316
Here, the stored mixed units are returned consistently in pounds.

69
00:06:10,316 --> 00:06:16,466
The cursor continues after the last entry using both time and ID.

70
00:06:17,850 --> 00:06:23,000
Personal records select three winners independently.

71
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:30,413
They are the heaviest set, highest volume set, and best estimated one repetition

72
00:06:30,413 --> 00:06:30,983
maximum.

73
00:06:30,983 --> 00:06:35,183
Each result includes when it was achieved.

74
00:06:36,558 --> 00:06:43,673
For this fixture, February Bench Press wins all three: seventy kilograms, five hundred

75
00:06:43,673 --> 00:06:50,241
sixty kilogram repetitions, and about eighty-eight point six six six seven kilograms.

76
00:06:51,558 --> 00:06:58,085
Compared with January, the increases are ten, eighty, and about twelve point six

77
00:06:58,085 --> 00:06:59,541
six six seven.

78
00:06:59,541 --> 00:07:03,841
The difference is comparison minus baseline.

79
00:07:05,225 --> 00:07:10,791
An empty history range returns a successful empty result.

80
00:07:10,791 --> 00:07:19,625
Invalid units, missing dates, negative values, and empty sets return structured validation errors.

81
00:07:19,625 --> 00:07:26,225
Each error includes a request ID, without exposing database internals.

82
00:07:27,600 --> 00:07:32,333
The CSV API accepts one row per set.

83
00:07:32,333 --> 00:07:39,019
This small file uses a separate user to keep the earlier record example

84
00:07:39,019 --> 00:07:39,533
unchanged.

85
00:07:40,933 --> 00:07:46,349
Upload returns an accepted response and an import ID.

86
00:07:46,349 --> 00:07:51,983
I use the status endpoint to follow that job until completion.

87
00:07:53,350 --> 00:07:58,633
The worker validates the whole file before writing workouts.

88
00:07:58,633 --> 00:08:03,016
Invalid input therefore creates no workouts.

89
00:08:03,016 --> 00:08:08,016
Valid files are then applied in bounded transactions.

90
00:08:09,392 --> 00:08:14,275
Each batch commits its workouts and progress together.

91
00:08:14,275 --> 00:08:18,625
A restart can continue from that checkpoint.

92
00:08:18,625 --> 00:08:24,925
However, a later permanent failure can leave earlier committed batches.

93
00:08:24,925 --> 00:08:32,492
CSV import therefore has different atomicity from a single JSON bulk request.

94
00:08:33,850 --> 00:08:37,766
Codex was my main coding assistant.

95
00:08:37,766 --> 00:08:45,233
I used it for design, implementation, testing, debugging, and documentation.

96
00:08:45,233 --> 00:08:52,583
My responsibility was to supply context, make decisions, and review the result.

97
00:08:53,975 --> 00:09:02,208
Before implementation, I organized project rules in AGENTS dot md, workflow documents, and

98
00:09:02,208 --> 00:09:03,425
coding standards.

99
00:09:03,425 --> 00:09:12,641
Task-specific skills supplied guidance for NestJS, PostgreSQL, validation, security, and review.

100
00:09:14,017 --> 00:09:21,383
My prompts included the assignment, the approved design, the current phase, and its

101
00:09:21,383 --> 00:09:21,900
boundaries.

102
00:09:21,900 --> 00:09:27,683
I settled important decisions before asking for implementation.

103
00:09:27,683 --> 00:09:35,033
Examples included atomic bulk writes, UTC periods, and retry behavior.

104
00:09:36,392 --> 00:09:43,731
I split work into phases and delegated bounded tasks, such as database tests

105
00:09:43,731 --> 00:09:45,375
and benchmark verification.

106
00:09:45,375 --> 00:09:49,892
A separate reviewer checked the source and tests.

107
00:09:49,892 --> 00:09:55,958
The tracked code and reports are the durable evidence because local plans are

108
00:09:55,958 --> 00:09:57,358
excluded from Git.

109
00:09:58,725 --> 00:10:03,475
The workflow log records two useful corrections.

110
00:10:03,475 --> 00:10:11,475
First, JavaScript and PostgreSQL normalized some Unicode exercise names differently.

111
00:10:11,475 --> 00:10:16,258
Ordinary English names did not reveal this problem.

112
00:10:16,258 --> 00:10:21,879
A name beginning with Turkish capital I with a dot could behave differently

113
00:10:21,879 --> 00:10:23,608
across the two layers.

114
00:10:25,017 --> 00:10:33,172
The correction made application normalization authoritative and added a migration to backfill existing

115
00:10:33,172 --> 00:10:33,750
values.

116
00:10:33,750 --> 00:10:39,050
The database regression includes Turkish I and Greek sigma.

117
00:10:39,050 --> 00:10:46,272
This test checks the actual database behavior rather than assuming two lowercase functions

118
00:10:46,272 --> 00:10:47,383
are equivalent.

119
00:10:48,767 --> 00:10:55,950
Second, a checked-out PostgreSQL connection could disconnect and crash the process.

120
00:10:55,950 --> 00:11:02,434
The pool listener covered idle clients, but an active client needed its own

121
00:11:02,434 --> 00:11:02,933
listener.

122
00:11:04,308 --> 00:11:10,174
The fix adds a scoped error listener and discards a damaged client.

123
00:11:10,174 --> 00:11:16,634
The integration test terminates its own database connection and checks that a later

124
00:11:16,634 --> 00:11:18,124
query still works.

125
00:11:18,124 --> 00:11:24,058
That is a concrete failure scenario, not just a mocked rejection.

126
00:11:25,433 --> 00:11:31,116
I also rejected treating retries as independent submissions.

127
00:11:31,116 --> 00:11:36,916
A network timeout does not tell the caller whether the server committed.

128
00:11:36,916 --> 00:11:41,299
Retrying must not create a second workout.

129
00:11:41,299 --> 00:11:47,533
That decision led to the required idempotency key and stored response.

130
00:11:48,933 --> 00:11:55,585
These examples show the review loop: identify a risk, reproduce it, correct the

131
00:11:55,585 --> 00:11:59,116
design or code, and verify the behavior.

132
00:11:59,116 --> 00:12:04,966
I do not claim that every test existed before its implementation.

133
00:12:04,966 --> 00:12:09,766
The workflow log makes that limitation explicit.

134
00:12:11,142 --> 00:12:16,242
I also checked the quality of the verification itself.

135
00:12:16,242 --> 00:12:21,958
Comparing only row counts could miss wrong weights or user ownership.

136
00:12:21,958 --> 00:12:29,108
The improved checks read stored values through a separate database client and use

137
00:12:29,108 --> 00:12:30,208
independent arithmetic.

138
00:12:30,208 --> 00:12:37,188
The verifier includes deliberate corruption cases to show that it can detect incorrect

139
00:12:37,188 --> 00:12:37,725
data.

140
00:12:39,142 --> 00:12:43,625
This complete file handles record arithmetic.

141
00:12:43,625 --> 00:12:47,092
Line one imports Decimal.

142
00:12:47,092 --> 00:12:52,225
Line two imports the metric and database winner types.

143
00:12:52,225 --> 00:12:59,280
Lines four to seven create a dedicated Decimal constructor with fifty significant digits

144
00:12:59,280 --> 00:13:00,908
and half-up rounding.

145
00:13:00,908 --> 00:13:06,208
This keeps arithmetic settings local to these calculations.

146
00:13:07,600 --> 00:13:11,033
Line eight defines set volume.

147
00:13:11,033 --> 00:13:16,683
Weight arrives as a string to preserve the database decimal value.

148
00:13:16,683 --> 00:13:22,950
Line nine multiplies weight by repetitions and returns a decimal string.

149
00:13:22,950 --> 00:13:30,250
Sixty kilograms for eight repetitions gives four hundred eighty kilogram repetitions.

150
00:13:30,250 --> 00:13:33,700
Line ten closes the function.

151
00:13:35,058 --> 00:13:39,691
The next function calculates the Epley estimate.

152
00:13:39,691 --> 00:13:43,608
Line twelve creates the decimal weight.

153
00:13:43,608 --> 00:13:48,874
Line thirteen multiplies by thirty plus repetitions.

154
00:13:48,874 --> 00:13:52,558
Line fourteen divides by thirty.

155
00:13:52,558 --> 00:13:59,012
This is the same formula as weight times one plus repetitions divided by

156
00:13:59,012 --> 00:13:59,508
thirty.

157
00:13:59,508 --> 00:14:05,774
Line fifteen returns the string, and line sixteen closes the function.

158
00:14:07,183 --> 00:14:12,249
This private helper builds a numerator for each metric.

159
00:14:12,249 --> 00:14:19,147
Lines eighteen to twenty-three choose one for heaviest weight, repetitions for volume, or

160
00:14:19,147 --> 00:14:21,799
thirty plus repetitions for Epley.

161
00:14:21,799 --> 00:14:27,199
Line twenty-four multiplies that value by normalized weight.

162
00:14:27,199 --> 00:14:31,566
Epley division is deliberately postponed.

163
00:14:31,566 --> 00:14:35,366
Line twenty-five closes the helper.

164
00:14:36,767 --> 00:14:41,500
The next function returns a winner's actual value.

165
00:14:41,500 --> 00:14:46,050
Lines twenty-seven and twenty-eight handle volume.

166
00:14:46,050 --> 00:14:50,233
Lines twenty-nine and thirty handle Epley.

167
00:14:50,233 --> 00:14:56,1000
Line thirty-one returns the original normalized weight for the heaviest-set metric.

168
00:14:56,1000 --> 00:15:00,833
Line thirty-two closes the function.

169
00:15:02,225 --> 00:15:07,358
Finally, this function accepts a metric and two winners.

170
00:15:07,358 --> 00:15:13,808
The comment explains why subtraction happens before division or display rounding.

171
00:15:13,808 --> 00:15:20,608
Line thirty-nine subtracts the baseline numerator from the comparison numerator.

172
00:15:20,608 --> 00:15:26,790
Lines forty to forty-two divide only an Epley difference by thirty, then return

173
00:15:26,790 --> 00:15:27,741
a string.

174
00:15:27,741 --> 00:15:31,575
Line forty-three closes the function.

175
00:15:32,975 --> 00:15:37,475
Winner selection happens in SQL elsewhere.

176
00:15:37,475 --> 00:15:42,358
This file only calculates values and differences.

177
00:15:42,358 --> 00:15:48,341
Keeping those responsibilities separate makes each easier to test.

178
00:15:49,725 --> 00:15:56,167
For our monthly example, the volume difference is five hundred sixty minus four

179
00:15:56,167 --> 00:15:57,108
hundred eighty.

180
00:15:57,108 --> 00:16:00,008
The result is eighty.

181
00:16:00,008 --> 00:16:07,923
Rounding earlier intermediate values could produce a different comparison, so final display formatting

182
00:16:07,923 --> 00:16:09,141
happens later.

183
00:16:10,517 --> 00:16:17,417
The repository contains unit tests and real PostgreSQL integration tests.

184
00:16:17,417 --> 00:16:26,650
They cover calculations, validation, rollback, retries, concurrent requests, and worker recovery.

185
00:16:28,017 --> 00:16:35,167
The workflow log records three hundred eighty-six unit tests and two hundred eighty-five

186
00:16:35,167 --> 00:16:36,767
integration tests passing.

187
00:16:36,767 --> 00:16:43,967
These are documented historical results, not a fresh test run during this video.

188
00:16:45,350 --> 00:16:52,066
Performance reports use fifty thousand entries and one hundred fifty thousand sets.

189
00:16:52,066 --> 00:17:00,112
After query improvements, the recorded all-time records p ninety-five was about one hundred

190
00:17:00,112 --> 00:17:01,350
thirty-one milliseconds.

191
00:17:01,350 --> 00:17:07,216
An independent integer-arithmetic oracle checked the responses.

192
00:17:08,600 --> 00:17:14,450
Those measurements came from a local environment at concurrency one.

193
00:17:14,450 --> 00:17:18,383
They demonstrate the tested workload.

194
00:17:18,383 --> 00:17:23,966
They do not prove production capacity for ten thousand coaches.

195
00:17:25,350 --> 00:17:31,283
For ten thousand concurrent coaches, I would first define the workload.

196
00:17:31,283 --> 00:17:36,616
Connected users are not the same as requests per second.

197
00:17:36,616 --> 00:17:43,457
I need the read and write mix, burst size, import volume, and acceptable

198
00:17:43,457 --> 00:17:43,983
latency.

199
00:17:45,350 --> 00:17:49,616
The API can run multiple replicas.

200
00:17:49,616 --> 00:17:56,483
Their combined database connection pools must remain within database capacity.

201
00:17:56,483 --> 00:18:04,427
I would measure pool saturation, lock waits, database CPU and input-output, and request

202
00:18:04,427 --> 00:18:07,483
latency before choosing infrastructure size.

203
00:18:08,850 --> 00:18:15,650
I would add authentication and tenant authorization before serving real client data.

204
00:18:15,650 --> 00:18:22,490
A user ID in a URL currently selects data; it does not establish

205
00:18:22,490 --> 00:18:23,016
permission.

206
00:18:24,392 --> 00:18:29,375
Imports need admission limits and worker supervision.

207
00:18:29,375 --> 00:18:34,375
The current design allows one worker per database.

208
00:18:34,375 --> 00:18:40,275
Multiple workers would require a tested claim and lease protocol.

209
00:18:40,275 --> 00:18:46,475
Retry keys and job metadata also need an agreed retention policy.

210
00:18:47,850 --> 00:18:54,183
I would tune measured query bottlenecks before adding caches or read replicas.

211
00:18:54,183 --> 00:19:00,650
Those additions need explicit freshness rules for recent workouts and records.

212
00:19:02,017 --> 00:19:07,650
I would then load test the expected traffic and failure scenarios against agreed

213
00:19:07,650 --> 00:19:13,283
latency and error budgets, and use those results to guide the next change.

214
00:19:14,600 --> 00:19:21,566
The implementation covers the assignment workflow, while these limits remain visible.

215
00:19:21,566 --> 00:19:24,333
Thank you for watching.
